WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.130
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:13.169
Today we are going to take a bad choppy video,
and make a beautiful, smooth and creamy footage

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:14.469
out of it.

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:21.040
With today's camera and graphics technology,
we can create videos with 60 frames per second.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.190
Those are really smooth, I also make each
of these videos using 60 frames per second,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:33.281
however, it almost always happens that I encounter
the paper videos that have only from 24 to

00:00:33.281 --> 00:00:35.430
30 frames per second.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:42.470
In this case, I put them in my video editor
that has a 60 fps timeline, where half or

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:46.350
even more of these frames will not provide
any new information.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.230
That’s neither smooth nor creamy.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.620
And it gets worse.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:52.620
Look!

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.790
As we try to slow down the videos for some
nice slow-motion action, this ratio becomes

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:04.159
even worse, creating an extremely choppy output
video because we have huge gaps between these

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:05.159
frames.

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:10.110
So, does this mean that there is nothing we
can do and have to put up with this choppy

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.240
footage?

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.450
No, not at all!

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:18.320
Look at this technique from 2019 that we covered
in an earlier video.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.600
The results truly speak for themselves.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:26.770
In goes a choppy video, and out comes a smooth,
and creamy result.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.530
So good!

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:35.170
But wait, it is not 2019, it is 2021, and
we always say that two more papers down the

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.700
line, and it will be improved significantly.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.530
From this example, it seems that we are done
here, we don’t need any new papers.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:44.840
Is that so?

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.640
Well, let’s see what we have only one more
paper down the line!

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.360
Now, look.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:58.090
It promises that it can deal with 10 to 1,
or even 20 to 1 ratios, which means that for

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:05.230
every single image in the video, it creates
10 or 20 new ones, and supposedly we shouldn’t

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:06.520
notice that.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.950
Well, those are big words, so I will believe
it when I see it.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:15.060
Let’s have a look together!

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.670
Holy mother of papers!

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:21.220
This can really pull this off, and it seems
nearly perfect.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.530
Wow.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.620
It also knocked it out of the park with this
one.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.950
And all this improvement in just one more
paper down the line.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:34.360
The pace of progress in machine learning research
is absolutely amazing.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:40.560
But, we are experienced Fellow Scholars over
here, so we will immediately ask, is this

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.150
really better than the previous 2019 paper?

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.150
Let’s compare them!

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:49.129
Can we have side by side comparisons?

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:50.819
Of course we can!

00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:52.890
You know how much I love fluid simulations.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:59.330
Well, these are not simulations, but a real
piece of fluid, and in this one, there is

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:00.330
no contest.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:06.420
The new one understands the flow so much better,
while the previous method sometimes even seems

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.450
to propagate the waves backwards in time.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.300
A big checkmark for the new one.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.030
In this case, the previous method assumes
linear motion when it shouldn’t, thereby

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:21.599
introducing a ton of artifacts.

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:26.700
The new one isn’t perfect either, but it
performs significantly better.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.250
Do not worry for a second, we will talk about
linear motion some more in a moment.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.849
So how does all this wizardry happen?

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:40.670
One of the key contributions of the paper
is that it can find out when to use the easy

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.750
way and the hard way.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:45.240
What are those?

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:51.540
The easy way is using already existing information
in the video and computing inbetween states

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.150
for a movement.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:58.400
That is all well and good if we have simple,
linear motion in our video.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.330
But, look, the easy way fails here.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.420
Why is that?

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:09.379
It fails because we have a difficult situation
where reflections off of this object rapidly

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:12.930
change, and it reflects something.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.440
We have to know what that something is.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.439
So, look, this is not even close to the true
image, which means that here, we can’t just

00:04:21.439 --> 00:04:27.039
reuse the information in the video, this requires
introducing new information.

00:04:27.039 --> 00:04:29.580
Yes, that is the hard way!

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.099
And this excels when new information has to
be synthesized.

00:04:33.099 --> 00:04:36.499
Let’s see how well it does!

00:04:36.499 --> 00:04:42.930
My goodness, look at that, it matches the
true reference image almost perfectly.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:49.020
And also, look, the face of the human did
not require synthesizing a great deal of new

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:54.520
information, it did not change over time,
so we can easily refer to the previous frame

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:59.660
for it, hence, the easy way did better here.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.330
Did you notice?

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:05.409
That is fantastic, because the two are complementary.

00:05:05.409 --> 00:05:09.289
Both techniques work well, but they work well
elsewhere.

00:05:09.289 --> 00:05:11.500
They need each other!

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:16.710
So, yes, you guessed right, to tie it all
together, there is also an attention-based

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:22.419
averaging step that helps us decide when to
use the easy, and the hard ways.

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:28.389
Now, this is a good paper, so it tells us
how these individual techniques contribute

00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:30.969
to the final image.

00:05:30.969 --> 00:05:37.139
Using only the easy way can give us about
26 decibels, that would not beat the previous

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:38.719
methods in this area.

00:05:38.719 --> 00:05:40.399
However, look!

00:05:40.399 --> 00:05:47.020
By adding the hard way, we get a premium quality
result that is already super competitive,

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:54.009
and, if we add the step that helps us decide
when to use the easy and hard ways, we get

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:55.699
an extra decibel.

00:05:55.699 --> 00:05:58.449
I will happily take it, thank you very much!

00:05:58.449 --> 00:06:06.639
And, if we put it all together, oh yes, we
get a technique that really outpaces the competition.

00:06:06.639 --> 00:06:07.900
Excellent.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:14.349
So, in the near future, perhaps we will be
able to record a choppy video of a family

00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:21.429
festivity, and have a chance at making this
choppy video enjoyable, or maybe even create

00:06:21.429 --> 00:06:25.809
slow-motion videos with a regular camera.

00:06:25.809 --> 00:06:28.699
No slow-motion camera is required.

00:06:28.699 --> 00:07:14.909
What a time to 
be alive!

00:07:14.909 --> 00:07:24.199
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

